refaction wave or an  expansion fan where the water is  smoothly decreasing in depth and  increasing in velocity as the water is  accelerated so again going back to the  beginning we start with this initial  condition and you get basically two  waves one a discontinuity moving to the  right the other sort of spreading fan  moving to the left and so that would be  the solution of this Ramon problem for  this particular case of the shallow  water equations okay so I've shown you  one particular solution of a Ramon  problem for this set of equations but  you might be wondering well what happens  if I change the data here and it's a  little hard with this I could have made  up some more slides that show other  things but it's even harder if you're  reading about this in a book and trying  to understand what goes on you might  like to be able to play around with the  conditions here so another thing I do in  addition to developing software and  working on these applications is I like  to write books or at least I've written  several over the last 30 years or so  they've been at UW as I sort of  developed course notes on various  aspects of these problems so I've  written several sort of old-fashioned  classical books on the topic of  hyperbolic problems and wave propagation  problems  there's lots of other books out there  they've been written by very good people  so good question is why are we writing  another book well the answer is that I  think the do clear note books have  really opened up completely new ways of  presenting this material and interacting  with it in ways that I think is very  valuable to students and researchers who  are trying to learn about all this so  there's lots of other books that have  been written by now using ghupat or  notebooks and we've looked at the number  of those and I encourage you if you  haven't looked at this gallery of  interesting Jupiter notebooks online you  can find lots of examples of various  notebooks but we've faced a few  challenges in dealing with the types of  problems that we're looking at in  particularly looking at sort of these  time-dependent solutions where we want  to see how things evolve in time that  we've been trying to address with some  of the work that that we're doing now so  what would we like out of these  notebooks well what we're trying to  develop is a collection of notebooks  that can be viewed as chapters in a book  that had cross referencing between the  chapters equation numbers that you can  refer back to bibliographic citations  all the things you would expect in a  book that also work well in the  notebooks and of course the notebooks  should be interactive so that people can  play around with things you'd like to  see this time evolution you'd like to be  able to play an animation or have a  slider bar to see how the ramond  solution evolves with time but you'd  also like to be able to change the  states to the left and the right and see  how that changes the solution or for  some systems and equations are various  physical parameters you'd like to play  around with and see how that changes the  solution so you want to be able to play  around with them but not everyone will  be running the notebooks or willing to  download and install them or even run  them in the cloud maybe so we'd also  like to produce from these notebooks a  set of HTML pages that are easier for  people to browse through without having  to actually run anything that maybe  won't have all the interactivity that  the notebook does but at least still has  some animations or things like that in  it and we also like to turn this into an  actual physical book we're hoping to  publish this in addition to having the  notebooks available online publishing it  as a book that might be useful to sit  down and read through and then go to the  note  when you want to play around with some  specific thing so we'd like to export  from the notebooks directly to a PDF  file that is reasonable as a printed  book and then also in addition to having  the notebooks available for people to  run on their own computer have them  hosted on the cloud so that people can  easily play around with them so those  are some of the things that we've been  sort of aiming for supporting so let me  switch to looking at a few of these  notebooks and then I'll talk at the end  abit about some of the tools we found  useful in doing this and--but as we go  along to see so if you go to that github  repository you can download these  notebooks if you want to play around  with them but there's one called index  which has sort of our working table of  contents anyway of the notebooks that we  have put together so far I should say  this is a joint project with two of my  former PhD students  David Ketchikan and Mauricio Durazo we  put a lot of work into this as well so  let me just show you a couple of  notebooks so first of all if we go to  the introduction here we describe what  this riemann problem is and we give some  examples including the sort of dam break  problem that i showed you on the slides  only here it's in a form where it's  using the interactive capabilities of  Jupiter so that you can use the time  slider here this particular version in  the notebook is really or in this  introduction is really designed to sort  of talk people through some of the basic  ideas so there's some other  corresponding figures down here that are  set up with different initial conditions  for example if it's deeper on the right  then the left then what you get is sort  of the waves in the opposite  configuration with a shock wave moving  to left and expansion wave moving to the  right or if you have water that's  flowing towards the middle from both  sides so you start with constant depth  but a positive velocity on one side and  negative velocity on the other then you  get two shockwaves moving out instead or  if you have flow that  flowing outwards on both sides then you  get to rarefaction waves for example so  this introduction there's some sort of  specified problems here but of course  the user could go in and modify things  here if they wanted to see what would  happen if we change the initial  conditions maybe make the depth two on  the left but keep everything else the  same then you can see how that changes  the solution of course it's nicer if you  have actually a bigger menu of options  that people can change directly so from  here we can also go into there's another  notebook on the chawla water equations  it's quite a long notebook actually that  describes much more about the theory and  has a number of things but down to the  very bottom there's a similar animation  but where you can now change the left  and right state with slider bars as well  as having a slider bar for the time so  one thing that we wanted to do as I said  is to make it possible to have these  sorts of things in the notebook but then  also produce from these HTML pages so  that people can just browse through them  without actually running them and also  eventually a PDF version so let me just  show you sort of what we've done there  if we go back to the introduction back  at the top there's an import statements  and in particular we import from I PI  widgets we import interact we then have  a script that turns these into HTML  pages by using MB convert and in the  process of doing that we also before  running and B convert we replace any  line that says from I PI widgets import  interacts with a different line which  using at the HTML version of this it's  been replaced by our own version of  interact from utils j/s animate widgets  import interact and this instead of  using the interactive widgets which  require actually running the notebook  instead uses the j/s animation package  developed by Jake van der glass which  basically takes a set of images and  combines them using some Java so  ripped into an animation that can be  played in the web browser and this can  be played now on the HTML page without  having to be running the notebook so  that's the way that we've so far found  to handle sort of taking some of these  interactive things and turning them into  a static HTML page but that still has at  least some of the interactive features  and then as I mentioned we also want to  turn it into a PDF file for a book and  so again you can use nd convert combined  with there's a package called book book  which helps to take a whole collection  of notebooks and turn them into chapters  in the book and so from all of the  notebooks we get a PDF file that has all  of the chapters and now if we go to that  same introduction what we see here make  this bigger is that in our conversion  the PDF we replace that interacts with a  yet another version of that function  from this snapshot would schatz widgets  module that we wrote we import interact  which basically takes the the widgets  that were defined and this interacts and  produces a static figure at one  particular value of the time so in the  widget in the interactive notebook we  specified an initial value of zero  because in the interactive notebook it  makes sense to start at time zero and  then as you slide the slider bar you see  time evolving for the for the PDF file  it wouldn't really make sense to only  show the initial conditions you wouldn't  see the structure of the solution so we  also have in our post-processing script  code that takes a value of zero any  place in these interacts and replaces it  with some slightly later time point two  or something that we've specified so  that we get a more interesting picture  so that's one thing that we've sort of  struggled with is how do we convert the  interacts directly to something that  worked looks nice in the PDF file or in  the HTML file now when I was at Berkeley  last week I was talking with Fernando  and M about maybe trying to automate  this more and building into the interact  within the notebook  additional parameters 