ory of the  riemann problem is understanding how to  compute the solution these curves that  are drawn here in the phase plane are  fundamental to figuring out given a left  and right state how do you find the  intermediate state this middle state or  certain curves intersect in between and  how do you determine whether each of the  waves is a shock wave or rarefaction  light and so that the notebooks and  textbooks that already exist go into  great detail about how to go about doing  that but from just reading a book you  don't get a very good feel for what  happens as you vary those those states  and so I think these kinds of tools make  it much easier I hope for students to  understand really what's going on and  how how the structure of these  mathematical problems evolves as you  change the data so that's the goal and  at the moment this which you can  actually embed within a notebook by just  displaying it as HTML but then we  haven't figured out how to translate  that into the PDF version for example so  we're still working on that aspect of it  let's see I guess I'll show one other  sort of menu of options that is this  kind of thing is relatively easy to do  in the jupiter notebook to describe  here's again a phase plane this is for  compressible gas dynamics problem where  you actually have conservation of mass  momentum and energy but it's just  projected onto the pressure and velocity  plane here and there's various things  you can change again you could change  the left and right state in this case  again with slider bars but you can also  change the parameters that determine the  us the equation of state for this  particular gas that you're looking at  and as you change those parameters that  changes the way these curves behave in  phase space or you can choose various  different plot options for how you want  to display the results again when we  render this as a PDF currently or as an  HTML file currently all that shows up  when you then be convert i